## New code review — no new defects above P3

**The `valueOf` change (`cbor.cljc:349-356`)** — sound. It sits in the `#?(:cljs (deftype Float64 ...))` block only (JVM/Dart compile nothing new), calls the existing `refuse` helper (`cbor.cljc:80-86`, throws `ex-info` with `::refusal :carrier-coercion` — matching the ruling's "raised through the codec's refusal helper"; public accessor `cbor/refusal` at `:96-99`), and leaves `toString`, `IEquiv`, `IHash`, `IPrintWithWriter` untouched. I hunted for surviving implicit-coercion consumers and found none: the encoder's `float-value` and `float-wire` read `.-v` directly (`cbor.cljc:532-537, 982-1012`); `data/float-value` and both de Bruijn bit encoders unwrap via `.-v` (`data.cljc:435`, `debruijn.cljc:873`, `debruijn_code.cljc:237`); `cbor/float64`'s `js/Number` is guarded by an `instance?` check (`cbor.cljc:501`); canonical map/set key ordering sorts encoded bytes, never the carriers (`cbor.cljc:1069, 1176`); map-key lookup and set membership use `IEquiv`/`IHash` and are tested (`cbor_test.cljc`, "every host" block). String conversion still reaches `toString` first (JS ToPrimitive order), so `str`/`String`/`pr-str` are unaffected as documented. Scope respected: Decimal and Rational carriers untouched. On JVM/Dart, generic arithmetic on float64 content (a native double) computes — the asymmetry is the disclosed, doc-pinned design, not a defect.

**The new tests can all fail** — they are real assertions, not tautologies: `float64-carrier-refuses-coercion-test` (`cbor_test.cljc:508-571`) enumerates all 8 operators × 3 operand positions (first/second/variadic), each required to throw with class `:carrier-coercion` and with `:yin.k/status` absent on JS, while computing the exact value on JVM/Dart; plus raw `'' + carrier` via `js*`, `js/Number`, `js/String`/`.toString`/`pr-str` printing, equality/hash/key/byte checks. `decoded-float-under-bare-plus-test` (`float_address_test.cljc:329-349`) drives a decoded carrier through a real `+` under all four runners — `[:thrown :carrier-coercion]` on Node, `3.0` on JVM/Dart — walking `ex-cause` via `refusal-in`, so exception re-wrapping can't hide the class. The lifecycle tests (`:447-455` lift, `:458-473` pin + GC sweep, `:506-551` closure with cross-task transfer under different capability secrets and continuation payload admission, all four VMs) compare `b64` of the seven specials against the originals — a byte drift fails them. The continuation non-lift limitation appears only as the accepted deviation (admission + payload bytes, docstring at `:507-510`). The rebase's fresh goldens (`:221,241-249`) pin the new prelude/A/A′ addresses, and `(= (prelude-id a) (prelude-id a'))` plus the unchanged-id assertion back the §8.5.5 "no capture sits inside the seam" claim.

**The `:cljd`-first reordering** — correct and behavior-preserving: `numeric-class` (`debruijn.cljc:227-239`), `float-bits` ×2 (`debruijn.cljc:876-886`, `debruijn_code.cljc:240-250`), `host-long?` and `host-double?` (`debruijn_code.cljc:334-353`) all now put `:cljd` first with the explanatory comment. The new base arm `(cbor/float64? v) :double` sits before the `number?` gate so a JS carrier classifies `:double` instead of falling to `nil`; on JVM/CLJD it is equivalent to the previous arms (incl. `java.lang.Float` → `:double` either way). The `host-double?` cljd simplification (dropping the ratio/bigint complement terms) is sound: cljd `Rational` and Dart `BigInt` are not `number?`. The `cbor` require in `debruijn.cljc:48` is present (round 1 noted it was missing there).

**Merged §8.5.5 text** — every code claim checks out: scalar admission at the five named places (`vm.cljc:959-1011`, `engine.cljc:633-642`, `values.cljc:193-195`, de Bruijn `numeric-class` and the `:double` image arm whose `double-le-hex` reads the payload, `debruijn_code.cljc:374,433`); the interim dict-key paragraph matches the implementation; the yield-reliance is disclosed as standing "until C2 yields inside prelude bodies"; the named test files/functions exist and pin what the text claims. The `dao.jing.cbor.md` and UCF edits are accurate, including "not a corpus class and not a lift outcome" and the Float64-only scope statement.

**`data/numeric-key` isolation** — intact for the C3-S2 deletion: exactly four code sites (`data.cljc:452-464, :504`; `prelude.cljc:89, :1260-1263`), both doc records (docstring "Interim…"; §8.5.5 "The dict key") name the same replacement plan, and no callers outside the float path or the test keys sets.

